# Passive voice makes the thing acted on the subject of the sentence. It
# names the actor, which grammar calls the agent, after `by` or not at
# all (`the lock must be held by the caller`, `the timeout can be
# configured`). A modal verb is a helping verb that marks possibility,
# ability, or obligation, such as `can`, `might`, and `must`. This tier
# reports a passive after a modal verb. The actor that such a passive
# hides is usually the reader or the caller, the person the sentence
# instructs (`you can configure the timeout`, `the caller must hold the
# lock`). The Optional tier reports every other passive, where naming
# the actor helps less often.
#
# The rule matches a modal verb, `be`, and a participle, the `-ing` or
# `-ed` form of a verb (`retried`, `written`). An adverb or `not` can
# sit after the modal verb or after `be` (`must not be copied`, `can be
# safely removed`).
#
# `will be` plus a participle stays out, because the future-tense rule
# reports the `will` in it. Its message asks for the present tense with
# the actor as the subject (`the kubelet uses the default handler`), so
# one fix clears the sentence. The future-tense rule skips `will not`
# and `will also`, so this rule reports those (`the file will not be
# read`). The roadmap words (`will be removed`) belong to the roadmap
# rule.
#
# The filter `<VERB, !@stative>` requires one token to be a verb and not
# one of the stative words. Stative words name a state rather than an
# action, and the tagger also marks them as verbs. The stative list holds
# a progressive, a form of `be` plus an `-ing` verb (`must be running`
# names a state). It also holds the participles that docs use as
# adjectives (`must be set`, `can be enabled`) and the naming idioms
# (`can be called name`).
#
# The tagger marks many participles as adjectives (`cached`, `retried`,
# `flushed`), so `missed` lists the common ones by hand. The `by` branch
# exists because the tagger misses others (`must be guarded by a lock`).
# It accepts any word the tagger does not mark as an adjective, adverb,
# noun, or preposition. Weir has no token class for a number, so the
# object of `by` must be a pronoun, a proper noun, or a determiner. A
# determiner is a word that introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or
# `this`. That skips a dimension (`64 by 64`) and the phrases where `by`
# names no actor (`by default`, `by hand`).
#
# Stative words still read as a passive when an agent follows them (`the
# flag can be set by the caller`), so `agentive` lists the stative words
# that take one. The agent must start with a determiner, pronoun, or
# proper noun, and the determiner must not open `by the time` or `by the
# way`. The `!-` glued after the verb skips a hyphenated compound whose
# first half the tagger marks as a verb (`must be read-only`). It
# matches at the end of a clause too, so `the file can be skipped.`
# still counts.
#
# It hit 1840 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled hits
# read clearer with the actor named (`you can pass values of this type`).
# The two misses named a state (`might be uninitialized`) or had no
# actor to name (`the environment in which the command would be run`).
expr stative [PROG, missing, done, set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, available, stuck, broken, gone, finished, lost, left, known, frozen, wedged, based, tied, built, made, fixed, live, called, named, meant, supposed, recommended, designed, intended, bound, referred, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr missed [cached, compiled, computed, configured, decoded, discarded, encoded, evicted, fetched, flushed, hashed, linted, normalized, parsed, patched, persisted, propagated, queued, redirected, reset, restarted, retried, revoked, rotated, sanitized, serialized, stripped, synced, tagged, truncated, validated]
expr verb [<VERB, !@stative>, @missed]
expr agentive [set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, broken, known, built, made, fixed, called, named, designed, intended, bound, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr agent [(DET ![time, way]), PRON, PROPN]
expr modal [must, should, can, cannot, could, may, might, will, would, shall, can't, won't, shouldn't, couldn't, wouldn't, mustn't]
expr pp [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]
expr ag [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)]
expr byform <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ>
expr main [(<@modal, !will> be @byform by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (@modal [not, ADV] be @byform by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (<@modal, !will> be @pp!-), (@modal [not, ADV] be @pp!-), (<@modal, !will> be @ag by @agent), (@modal [not, ADV] be @ag by @agent)]
let message "Passive voice. Name who acts, such as `you can configure the timeout` or `the caller must hold the lock`."
let description "Flags a passive after a modal verb, such as `can be configured` or `must be held`, which hides who can or must act."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "You can configure the timeout."
allows "The job is retried."
allows "The cache might be stale."
allows "The flag will be removed in a future release."
allows "The flag will be added in a future release."
allows "The default handler will be used."
allows "The flag must be set."
allows "The flag can be enabled by default."
allows "The image can be 64 by 64 pixels."
allows "The lock must be read-only while the sweep runs."
allows "The job should be running by now."
